{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"rent_increase_limits","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Newly Constructed Multiple Dwellings Law rent control exemption","requirement":"A newly constructed multiple dwelling is exempt from any local rent control or rent leveling ordinance for a period of 30 years following completion of construction of the building.","key_value":"30 years","coverage_conditions":"Newly constructed multiple dwellings; exempt from any local rent control ordinance for 30 years after completion of construction.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":"construction_date","min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"Newly constructed multiple dwellings"},"exemptions":"Rents subsidized by governmental funding may also be exempt from local rent control ordinances.","penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:42-84.5","quoted_span":"Under the Newly Constructed Multiple Dwellings Law ( N.J.S.A. 2A:42 -84.5), newly
constructed multiple dwellings shall be exempt from any local rent control ordinances for a period
of 30 years following completion of construction of the building.","interaction":"Newly constructed dwellings are exempt from local rent control ordinances, which municipalities may adopt.","confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"rent_increase_limits","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Rent increase notice and unconscionability rule","requirement":"To raise the rent a landlord must terminate the old lease, offer a new lease at the higher rent and give proper written notice; rent may not be raised mid-lease, and an increase must not be unconscionable or violate a local rent ordinance.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"Residential tenancies; an increase must not be unconscionable and must comply with any municipal ordinance governing rent increases.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":null},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.1","quoted_span":"The increase in rent must not be unconscionable; it must not be so unreasonable as to shock the conscience of a fair and honest person and must comply with any municipal ordinances governing rent increases.","interaction":"Rent increases must comply with municipal rent control ordinances where they exist.","confidence":0.75,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"rent_increase_limits","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Conversion rent increase prohibition","requirement":"When a building is converted to condominium, cooperative or fee simple ownership of units, rents may not be increased to cover costs resulting solely from the conversion.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"All tenants in a building converted to condominium, cooperative or fee simple ownership, whether or not they qualify for protected tenancy.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"Buildings converted to condominium, cooperative or fee simple ownership"},"exemptions":"Rents may still be increased to cover the cost of new services or amenities.","penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.31","quoted_span":"If a building is converted to a condominium or cooperative form of ownership, or to fee
simple ownership of units, rents may not be increased to cover cos ts resulting solely from the
conversion (N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.31).","interaction":null,"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Anti-Eviction Act good cause requirement","requirement":"A landlord may recover possession of a residential dwelling only with the tenant's consent or through the legal eviction process and must have good cause to evict. Each cause except nonpayment of rent must be described in a written notice to quit.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"Dwelling units used as a residence; applies to written and oral leases. A Notice to Quit is required for all good cause evictions except nonpayment of rent.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":null},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.1","quoted_span":"No residential landlord may evict or fail to renew a
lease, whether it is a written or an oral lease without good cause. The landlord must be able to
prove in court that he has grounds for an eviction.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"security_deposits","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Security deposit duties of a successor owner","requirement":"When a rental property is sold or transferred, the new owner must obtain the tenant's security deposit and accrued interest and is responsible for investing the deposit, giving all notices, paying interest and returning the deposit plus accrued earnings.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"Residential rental security deposits collected by a former owner and any accrued interest, whether or not the deposit and interest are transferred.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":null},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 46:8-21","quoted_span":"Whether or not the deposit and interest are transferred, t he new owner is responsible for the
investment of the security deposit, giving all notices and paying interest, and for the return of the
security deposit, plus any accrued earnings or interest (N.J.S.A. 46:8-21).","interaction":null,"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"security_deposits","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Penalty for withholding deposits of tenants receiving financial assistance","requirement":"A landlord who willfully and intentionally withholds a security deposit made by or on behalf of a tenant receiving State or federal financial assistance, including welfare or rental assistance, may be liable for a civil penalty of $500 to $2,000 per offense.","key_value":"$500 to $2,000 per offense","coverage_conditions":"Security deposits made by or on behalf of tenants who receive State or federal financial assistance, including welfare or rental assistance.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":null},"exemptions":null,"penalty":"Civil penalty of not less than $500 or not more than $2,000 for each offense, collected by summary proceeding under the Penalty Enforcement Law; the State entity that made the deposits receives the penalty amounts. Unlawfully using security deposit monies may be charged as a disorderly person, with a fine of not less than $200 or imprisonment up to 30 days, or both (N.J.S.A. 46:8-25).","effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 46:8-21.1","quoted_span":"The landlord may be liable for a civil penalty of not less than $500 or not more than $2,000 for each offense.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"screening_restrictions","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Law Against Discrimination source-of-income protection","requirement":"The Law Against Discrimination bars a landlord or rental agency from refusing to rent or discriminating in housing because of source of lawful income, among other protected traits. It covers all landlord-tenant relationships except two-family owner-occupied dwellings and rooms in an owner-occupied single home.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"All landlord-tenant relationships, except two-family owner-occupied dwellings, rooms in an owner- or resident-occupied single home, and residences planned exclusively for and occupied by one sex (i.e., YMCA and age-restricted housing as to familial status).","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":true,"other":"Exempts owner-occupied two-family dwellings and rooms in owner-occupied single homes"},"exemptions":"Two-family owner-occupied dwellings; rooms in an owner- or resident-occupied single home; residences planned exclusively for and occupied by one sex, including YMCA and age-restricted housing as to familial status.","penalty":"Landlords who violate the LAD are subject to substantial fines and penalties, up to $10,000 for a first offense (N.J.S.A. 10:5-14.1a(a)); monetary damages may be awarded and the unit may be rented to the complainant.","effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 10:5-12","quoted_span":"The New Jersey Law Against Discrimination (LAD), N.J.S.A. 10:5-12(g) to -(h), prohibits discrimination when selling or renting property and requires equal treatment in the sale or rental of housing regardless of race, creed, color, national origin, ancestry, sex, marital status, civil union status, domestic partnership status, familial status, affectional or sexual orientation, gender  identity or expression, mental and physical disability,
nationality, or source of lawful income.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.7,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
